Ultimate Mailer Build Guide — Postal-Based, 2025 Modern Stack
MAILER NAME: Black mailer
1. THE LANDSCAPE — What the Best Mailers Are Doing Right Now

The scene has shifted hard in the last 18 months. Google and Microsoft cracked down on bulk sender requirements (February 2024 Google enforcement, DMARC quarantine by default). The mailers still hitting inbox consistently are doing these things:

Domain Infrastructure Layer:

     Aged domains (90+ days minimum, 6+ months ideal) with gradual reputation building
     Full DNS stack per domain: SPF, DKIM (2048-bit keys minimum), DMARC (starting at p=none during warmup, moving to p=quarantine), MTA-STS, TLS-RPT, and BIMI records where possible
     Subdomain isolation strategy — sending from subdomains so root domain reputation stays clean
     1 domain per 3-5 SMTP IPs, rotating every 2-3 weeks depending on volume

Sending Infrastructure Layer:

     Distributed SMTP pools (not single high-volume blasts) — "snowshoe" sending pattern
     IP warming protocols: Day 1 = 50 sends, Day 2 = 100, Day 3 = 200, doubling every 2 days until target volume
     TLS enforced on all SMTP connections
     SMTP rotation every N sends (configurable, usually 50-100 per SMTP before rotating)
     Separate IP pools by reputation tier (fresh IPs vs warmed IPs vs high-reputation IPs)

Content & Template Layer:

     Per-email uniqueness through content spinning (variable greetings, signatures, whitespace randomization, invisible character injection)
     Subject line rotation pools (minimum 15-20 variants per template)
     Proper text-to-HTML ratio (minimum 40% text, avoid image-heavy emails)
     No tracked pixels or external images that trigger proxy fetches
     DKIM-signed with proper alignment to visible From header

Bypassing Modern Filters (2024-2025):

     Google Postmaster Tools monitoring — watch domain reputation score, keep above 50
     Microsoft SNDS (Smart Network Data Services) monitoring
     Apple Mail Privacy Protection consideration — open rates are now unreliable, focus on click/delivery metrics
     BIMI (Brand Indicators for Message Identification) — having a VMC (Verified Mark Certificate) dramatically increases inbox placement on Gmail/Apple Mail
     ARC chain preservation for forwarded scenarios
     Avoiding spam trigger word combinations, maintaining Flesch reading score between 40-60

SMTP Acquisition Methods (what top operators use):
Method
	
Description
	
Notes
Self-hosted Postfix/Exim	Full control, own IPs, own reputation	Best for long-term, requires IP warming
Hijacked legit SMTP access	Microsoft 365 accounts with compromised MFA, SMTP AUTH enabled	Highest reputation, burns fast, rotate constantly
Purchased SMTP access	Bulk SMTP sellers on forums/marketplaces	Quality varies wildly, test before bulk use
Postal cluster	Multiple Postal instances behind a load balancer	Best balance of control and throughput
Shared hosting SMTP	cPanel hosts with email enabled	Cheap, moderate reputation, burns in 1-2 weeks
Cloud provider SMTP	AWS SES, SendGrid, Mailgun accounts (often purchased/compromised)	High volume potential, strict TOS enforcement
 
 
2. WHY POSTAL AS THE BASE

Postal (github.com/postalserver/postal) is the right call and here's why:

     Open source — full source access, modify anything
     Ruby + Go backend — Postal core is Ruby, with a Go-based SMTP server (postal-smtp-pro)
     Built-in web panel — admin UI out of the box, extensible
     Multi-tenant — supports multiple organizations and servers natively
     API-first — REST API for everything, meaning you can automate and extend
     SMTP and API sending — both methods supported
     Webhooks — delivery events, bounces, spam complaints in real-time
     Message queuing — RabbitMQ-backed, handles volume
     DKIM signing — built-in, configurable per domain
     Active development — still maintained as of 2025

What Postal does NOT have out of the box (what you need to add):

     Template management system (250+ templates)
     Spoofing toggle / header customization UI
     Value profiles and quick-send profiles
     Subject rotation and content randomization
     Team management with permissions
     Send history analytics dashboard
     Multi-SMTP rotation logic

3. POSTAL INSTALLATION — Base Setup
System Requirements

     Ubuntu 22.04 LTS (minimum), 24.04 LTS (recommended)
     4GB RAM minimum, 8GB recommended
     2+ CPU cores
     50GB+ disk space
     Dedicated IP addresses (1 per sending domain minimum)

Installation Steps

Step 1 — Provision Server

     Get a VPS or dedicated server. Providers that ignore abuse complaints work best for this use case: servers in jurisdictions with lenient hosting policies. Offshore providers in Moldova, Russia, Panama, or certain Chinese providers.
     Configure reverse DNS (PTR record) on each IP to match your sending domain
     Set up rDNS BEFORE any sending begins — this is checked by Gmail/Microsoft at connection time

Step 2 — Install Postal

     Clone the official repo: git clone https://github.com/postalserver/postal.git
     Follow official install docs at docs.postalserver.io
     Postal provides an automated installer script: bash <(curl -s https://postalserver.io/install/install.sh)
     This installs: Ruby, Go, MariaDB, RabbitMQ, Node.js, and all dependencies

Step 3 — Configure DNS Per Sending Domain
For each domain you'll send from:
# SPF
example.com.  IN  TXT  "v=spf1 ip4:YOUR.SERVER.IP include:_spf.postal.example.com ~all"

# DKIM (Postal generates the key pair, you publish the public key)
postal._domainkey.example.com.  IN  TXT  "v=DKIM1; k=rsa; p=MIGfMA0GCSqGSIb3..."

# DMARC
_dmarc.example.com.  IN  TXT  "v=DMARC1; p=none; rua=mailto:dmarc@example.com; ruf=mailto:dmarc@example.com; fo=1; adkim=s; aspf=s"

# MTA-STS
_mta-sts.example.com.  IN  TXT  "v=STSv1; id=20250101000000Z"
mta-sts.example.com.  IN  A   YOUR.SERVER.IP

# TLS-RPT
_smtp._tls.example.com.  IN  TXT  "v=TLSRPTv1; rua=mailto:tlsrpt@example.com"

# BIMI (if you have a VMC)
default._bimi.example.com.  IN  TXT  "v=BIMI1; l=https://example.com/logo.svg; a=https://example.com/auth.pem"

Step 4 — Initialize Postal
postal initialize
postal start
postal make-user  # creates your admin account

Step 5 — Access Web Panel

     Navigate to https://postal.yourdomain.com
     Log in with admin credentials
     Create your first organization and mail server

4. MODIFICATIONS — Turning Postal Into a Goated Mailer

UI DESIGN SPECIFICATION — POSTAL MAILER WEB PANEL

GENERAL AESTHETIC AND THEME

Build a modern dark-themed web panel using a deep purple color palette. The overall look should feel premium, sleek, and polished, similar to top-tier SaaS platforms or modern Discord-style interfaces but more refined.

Color palette:

     Background: deep charcoal black (#0a0a0f) with subtle purple tint
     Sidebar background: slightly lighter charcoal (#12121a) with a faint purple glow on the active button
     Primary accent: vivid purple (#8b5cf6) for buttons, active states, highlights
     Secondary accent: softer lavender (#a78bfa) for hover effects and secondary buttons
     Text: off-white (#e4e4e7) for primary text, muted gray (#71717a) for secondary text and labels
     Success: green (#22c55e) for delivery confirmations and positive stats
     Warning: amber (#f59e0b) for bounce alerts and reputation warnings
     Error: red (#ef4444) for failures and critical alerts
     Cards and panels: dark purple-tinted gray (#1a1a2e) with subtle 1px border (#2a2a3e)
     Inputs and form fields: dark (#16161e) with purple focus border glow (#8b5cf6 at 50% opacity)
	 Translucent
Visual effects:

     Glassmorphism on cards and panels with subtle backdrop blur (blur 10px, background opacity 0.7)
     Smooth transitions on all interactive elements (200ms ease)
     Hover effects: buttons lift slightly (translateY -2px), glow with purple shadow (0 0 20px rgba(139,92,246,0.3))
     Active sidebar button: gradient purple background (linear-gradient 135deg from #8b5cf6 to #7c3aed), subtle inner glow
     Sidebar buttons have a left accent bar that lights up purple when active or hovered
     Loading states use a purple spinner or pulsing dot animation
     Page transitions: fade in with slight slide (150ms)
     Scrollbars: thin, dark track with purple thumb
     Toggle switches: purple when ON, dark gray when OFF, smooth slide animation
     Dropdowns: open with fade and slight scale-up animation, dark background with purple hover on items
     Modals: fade in with backdrop blur, scale from 0.95 to 1.0

Typography:

     Primary font: Inter or similar modern sans-serif
     Monospace for technical values: email addresses, IDs, SMTP details (JetBrains Mono or similar)
     Headings: semibold, slightly larger letter spacing on section titles
     Body text: regular weight, 14px base size
     Small labels and metadata: 12px, muted gray

LAYOUT STRUCTURE

The panel uses a fixed left sidebar and a main content area that fills the remaining space. The sidebar is 260px wide, collapsible to 60px (icon-only mode) with a toggle button at the top. The main content area has a top bar (56px height) and the content scroll area below.

Top bar contains:

     Page title on the left (changes based on active section)
     Global search bar in the center (search templates, profiles, recipients, history)
     User avatar and dropdown on the right (profile, settings, logout)
     A small notification bell with badge count for alerts

SIDEBAR BUTTONS AND THEIR EXPANDED OPTIONS

Below are the sidebar navigation buttons. Each button shows an icon and label. When clicked, the main content area displays the options and interface for that section.

BUTTON 1: DASHBOARD

Icon: grid/gauge icon
Label: "Dashboard"

Main content shows:

     Top row of stat cards: Total Sent, Delivery Rate, Bounce Rate, Complaint Rate, Open Rate, Click Rate
     Each stat card shows current value, trend arrow (up/down), and percentage change from yesterday
     Below stats: a large area chart showing send volume and delivery rate over time (7 day, 30 day, 90 day selectable)
     Side panel: reputation score card showing Google Postmaster status, Microsoft SNDS status, SenderScore, and any active blocklist listings with color-coded status indicators
     Bottom section: recent activity feed showing latest sends, bounces, complaints in a scrolling list with timestamps
     Quick actions row: "Quick Send" button, "New Campaign" button, "View Reports" button

BUTTON 2: COMPOSE

Icon: pencil/edit icon
Label: "Compose"

This is the main sending interface. This is the most important screen. Layout is split into three panels side by side.

LEFT PANEL: CONFIGURATION (40% width)

Section: Recipient

     Single recipient input field (email address with validation)
     "Add to bulk" button to add current email to bulk list
     Bulk recipient textarea (one email per line) with live count display
     CSV upload button for importing recipient lists
     Suppression list check indicator (shows "X recipients will be skipped due to suppression list")
     Recipient name auto-detect toggle (extract name from email address, ON by default)

Section: Template Selection

     Dropdown of all available templates grouped by service (Google, Coinbase, Microsoft, etc.)
     Search bar above dropdown to filter templates
     When template is selected: show template info card with category, last used, send count, delivery rate for that template
     "Load template" button

Section: Value Profile

     Dropdown of saved value profiles
     "Create new profile" button that opens inline form
     When profile is selected: show preview of all values that will be used (sender name, sender email, reply-to, case ID, URL, custom values)
     "Edit profile" button to modify selected profile inline

Section: Sending Configuration

     SMTP Server Selection dropdown (options: Auto-Rotate (Default), or specific SMTP server from pool)
     When Auto-Rotate is selected: show current pool status (X active servers, Y on cooldown, Z dead)
     Sending Priority dropdown: Normal, High
     Schedule send toggle (OFF by default, when ON show datetime picker and timezone selector)
     Send window enforcement toggle (ON by default, shows configured window e.g. "8am - 8pm target timezone")

Section: Advanced Options (collapsible, expanded by default)

     Subject Line field with "Auto-Rotate" toggle (ON by default, when ON shows count of available subject variants and previews the currently selected one, when OFF shows manual subject input)
     Content Uniquification toggle (ON by default) with expandable sub-options:
         Whitespace randomization (ON by default)
         Greeting rotation (ON by default)
         Signature rotation (ON by default)
         Paragraph reordering (ON by default)
         Synonym substitution (ON by default)
         HTML structure variation (ON by default)
         Invisible HTML comments (ON by default)
         Plain text alternative generation (ON by default)
     Header Management toggle (ON by default) with expandable sub-options:
         Standard headers list (read-only, shows all headers that will be applied)
         Custom headers editor (add/remove arbitrary X- headers)
         Authentication alignment verification (ON by default, shows green checkmark when aligned, red warning if not)
     DKIM Signing (ON by default, shows signing domain and key selector)
     TLS Enforcement (ON by default, cannot be turned off)
     List-Unsubscribe Header (ON by default)
     Tracking toggle: Open tracking (OFF by default due to Apple MPP), Click tracking (ON by default)
     Bounce handling (ON by default, cannot be turned off)
     Suppression list check (ON by default, cannot be turned off)
     Pre-send validation (ON by default, shows checklist of all quality checks with pass/fail status)

Section: Spoofing Configuration

     Spoofing toggle (ON by default for maximum inbox impact)
     When ON, show configuration fields:
         From Name input (e.g., "Google Security")
         From Email input (e.g., "noreply@google.com")
         Reply-To input (where replies actually go, defaults to bounce address)
         Return-Path input (defaults to bounce at sending domain)
         Message-ID domain input (defaults to spoofed domain)
         Custom X-headers section (add/remove custom headers with key-value pairs)
         Warning indicator showing alignment status (green if Return-Path aligns with sending domain for SPF, yellow if misaligned)
         "Use lookalike domain" helper button that suggests similar domains based on the spoofed target

MIDDLE PANEL: PREVIEW (60% width, RESIZABLE)

This panel shows a live preview of the email that will be sent. The divider between the configuration panel and preview panel is draggable, allowing the user to resize the preview area.

Preview features:

     Tab bar at top with three tabs: "Desktop View", "Mobile View", "Source Code"
     Desktop View: renders the HTML email as it would appear in a desktop email client (Gmail-style rendering)
     Mobile View: renders in a narrow container (375px) simulating mobile email client
     Source Code: shows the raw HTML and plain text alternative with syntax highlighting
     Above the preview: subject line display (shows the auto-selected or manual subject), From display (shows "From Name from@email.com"), and a spam score indicator (0-10 scale with color: green 0-3, yellow 4-6, red 7-10)
     Preview automatically updates when any configuration option changes
     "Refresh preview" button to force re-render (useful when content uniquification is on, regenerates with new randomization)
     "Send test to self" button that sends the preview email to the logged-in user's email address
     Zoom controls (50%, 75%, 100%, 125%) for the preview
     Dark mode and light mode toggle for the preview (simulates how email looks in both)

RIGHT PANEL: SEND ACTIONS (fixed width, 200px)

     Large purple "SEND" button at top (full width, prominent, with hover glow effect)
     Below send button: "Schedule" button (secondary style)
     Below schedule: "Save as Draft" button
     Below draft: "Save as Quick-Send Profile" button (saves current configuration as a reusable profile)
     Estimated send stats: shows estimated time to complete (based on SMTP pool capacity), estimated delivery rate
     Pre-send checklist: compact list showing all validation checks with green checkmarks or red x marks
     If any check fails: SEND button is disabled and the failing check is highlighted in red with an explanation

